WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.050
Welcome to Part 1 of the implementation
series for provider notices.

2
00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:09.112
One thing first: Provider Notices,
An Implementation Guide, is a draft.

3
00:00:09.112 --> 00:00:13.038
It's awaiting the owner's review, so
check it again after that review.

4
00:00:13.038 --> 00:00:15.425
The guide's premise fits in one line.

5
00:00:15.425 --> 00:00:18.829
Every provider tells you, in
writing and ahead of time,

6
00:00:18.829 --> 00:00:22.000
what it will change, retire
or patch in your tenant.

7
00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.663
The gap is that nobody reads
it, owns it or closes it.

8
00:00:25.663 --> 00:00:28.451
The guide closes that gap in seven gates.

9
00:00:28.451 --> 00:00:33.374
Part 1 covers the orientation and
the four collection gates for Azure

10
00:00:33.374 --> 00:00:38.638
Service Health, Microsoft 365, Oracle
Cloud Infrastructure and AWS Health.

11
00:00:38.638 --> 00:00:41.924
Part 2 covers Google
Cloud, routing to owners,

12
00:00:41.924 --> 00:00:46.188
the digest and the evidence,
then the dangers and rollback.

13
00:00:46.188 --> 00:00:50.622
For each gate you'll see the steps, what
the kit really prints on its fixtures,

14
00:00:50.622 --> 00:00:54.413
the federal requirement, the
evidence and the government cloud.

15
00:00:54.413 --> 00:01:00.280
The kit's code is illustrative, tested
against fixtures, not against a live tenant.

16
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:05.380
Why does this need a guide?

17
00:01:05.380 --> 00:01:09.217
Because the workbook mentions provider
notices but doesn't run them.

18
00:01:09.217 --> 00:01:13.980
The day-two operations book routes Microsoft's
service health email to the help desk

19
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.633
supervisor, and warns
that nothing is approved

20
00:01:16.633 --> 00:01:18.742
or changed on the strength of an email.

21
00:01:18.742 --> 00:01:21.411
The security operations book sends Azure's

22
00:01:21.411 --> 00:01:24.229
service health log
category to the workspace.

23
00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:29.118
Neither collects the notices, assigns
an owner, or tracks one to closure.

24
00:01:29.118 --> 00:01:32.380
So the goal, which is
design guidance, is a loop.

25
00:01:32.380 --> 00:01:36.417
Every notice is collected on a
schedule and held as one record.

26
00:01:36.417 --> 00:01:41.431
It either goes to an owner as a task with
a due date, or into a weekly digest.

27
00:01:41.431 --> 00:01:44.655
And a task closes only when
the next collection shows

28
00:01:44.655 --> 00:01:47.880
the notice closed or the
resource no longer affected.

29
00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:51.460
That's the re-read, never
someone's word that it's done.

30
00:01:55.210 --> 00:02:00.634
The kit turns every provider's notice
into one record with one of six kinds:

31
00:02:00.634 --> 00:02:05.435
maintenance, retirement, advisory,
security, change and incident.

32
00:02:05.435 --> 00:02:08.560
The table maps each provider's
own types onto them.

33
00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.473
Two rows are worth a closer look.

34
00:02:10.473 --> 00:02:15.241
A retirement in Azure is a health advisory
with the subtype retirement, and in

35
00:02:15.241 --> 00:02:20.234
AWS the kit infers it from a scheduled
change whose event code names a retirement.

36
00:02:20.234 --> 00:02:23.572
That second one is the
kit's rule, not AWS's.

37
00:02:23.572 --> 00:02:26.473
And Google Cloud's column is almost empty:

38
00:02:26.473 --> 00:02:31.336
its Personalized Service Health API
has a single category, incident.

39
00:02:31.336 --> 00:02:36.322
Each record also says whether action
is required, by when, and who said so.

40
00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:41.748
Microsoft 365, Oracle and AWS
each have a field for that.

41
00:02:41.748 --> 00:02:46.378
Azure doesn't, so the kit applies
its own stated rule: a retirement is

42
00:02:46.378 --> 00:02:51.380
due at the advisory's end, which
Microsoft Learn says is its end time.

43
00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.847
Here are the five providers side by side,

44
00:02:57.847 --> 00:03:01.305
including Oracle Cloud,
where the HR platform runs.

45
00:03:01.305 --> 00:03:03.881
Each keeps its notices in its own place:

46
00:03:03.881 --> 00:03:07.020
Azure's Service Health
through Resource Graph,

47
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:12.091
Microsoft 365's message center and
service health through Microsoft Graph,

48
00:03:12.091 --> 00:03:15.472
Oracle's Announcements
service, the AWS Health API,

49
00:03:15.472 --> 00:03:18.692
and Google Cloud's
Personalized Service Health.

50
00:03:18.692 --> 00:03:23.114
The least-privileged reader is
small everywhere: Reader in Azure,

51
00:03:23.114 --> 00:03:26.961
two read permissions in Graph,
a read statement in Oracle,

52
00:03:26.961 --> 00:03:31.218
three describe actions in AWS,
and a viewer role in Google Cloud.

53
00:03:31.218 --> 00:03:35.243
Only AWS has a cost: a
qualifying support plan.

54
00:03:35.243 --> 00:03:41.187
On government clouds, Microsoft 365 GCC
stays on the worldwide Graph endpoint,

55
00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:45.943
while GCC High and the Defense
Department have their own hosts.

56
00:03:45.943 --> 00:03:49.517
Oracle's US Government
Cloud has its own endpoints.

57
00:03:49.517 --> 00:03:51.656
AWS GovCloud has one.

58
00:03:51.656 --> 00:03:54.810
And Google's documentation
doesn't list Personalized

59
00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:58.480
Service Health in its FedRAMP
Moderate or High packages.

60
00:04:02.230 --> 00:04:04.505
Before the first gate, three things.

61
00:04:04.505 --> 00:04:09.505
Decisions first: who owns each provider's
notices, which notices become tasks,

62
00:04:09.505 --> 00:04:14.817
for which group and at which severity, and
who receives each provider's email today.

63
00:04:14.817 --> 00:04:17.080
That routing belongs to the agency.

64
00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:21.149
It's written at work from the
kit's template, with its prompts in

65
00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:25.617
Microsoft 365 Copilot Chat, and it
never goes in a public repository.

66
00:04:25.617 --> 00:04:28.768
Second, a change process
that takes the tasks.

67
00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:32.826
The router writes ServiceNow-shaped
payloads, and until the agency

68
00:04:32.826 --> 00:04:36.667
builds an endpoint for them, they're
files the service desk reads.

69
00:04:36.667 --> 00:04:40.880
Third, a reader per provider,
holding only its read role.

70
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:44.992
The roles come from each provider's
own documentation, and they're short.

71
00:04:44.992 --> 00:04:49.052
The writes, meaning the Azure
Policy assignment and rollback,

72
00:04:49.052 --> 00:04:53.790
run as a different identity, and
only with an explicit apply switch.

73
00:04:57.540 --> 00:04:59.777
Here are all seven gates on one page.

74
00:04:59.777 --> 00:05:04.911
The first five are collection, one
per provider: Azure Service Health,

75
00:05:04.911 --> 00:05:10.315
Microsoft 365, Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,
AWS Health and Google Cloud.

76
00:05:10.315 --> 00:05:14.528
They're independent, so enable only
the providers the agency uses.

77
00:05:14.528 --> 00:05:19.302
Gate six routes what they collect: every
notice that needs action gets a task,

78
00:05:19.302 --> 00:05:21.878
and a second run doesn't
make a second task.

79
00:05:21.878 --> 00:05:24.252
Gate seven schedules the weekly run,

80
00:05:24.252 --> 00:05:28.765
sends the digest to the owners and
checks the evidence for each stage.

81
00:05:28.765 --> 00:05:32.315
Each gate is validated with
one of the kit's entry scripts.

82
00:05:32.315 --> 00:05:34.652
Every one of those checks only reads.

83
00:05:34.652 --> 00:05:37.352
The only writes are in
the kit's deploy folder,

84
00:05:37.352 --> 00:05:41.291
and they run only with an apply
switch and the plan's approved hash.

85
00:05:41.291 --> 00:05:46.170
And the checks are tested against
fixtures, not proven against a live tenant.

86
00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:52.070
Gate one is Azure Service Health.

87
00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:56.169
Resource Graph answers the same
questions for every subscription

88
00:05:56.169 --> 00:06:00.195
in scope with one query, and
Microsoft Learn publishes the queries.

89
00:06:00.195 --> 00:06:01.207
Four steps.

90
00:06:01.207 --> 00:06:04.163
A subscription owner grants
the reader two roles:

91
00:06:04.163 --> 00:06:06.758
Reader, and Service
Health Security Reader.

92
00:06:06.758 --> 00:06:10.925
Learn explains why the second one
matters: with Reader alone you see a

93
00:06:10.925 --> 00:06:15.532
security advisory, but not its sensitive
fields or the resources it affects.

94
00:06:15.532 --> 00:06:19.658
Then sign in to the right cloud,
with the Resource Graph extension.

95
00:06:19.658 --> 00:06:24.558
The workbook's estate is GCC with
commercial Azure, so that's Azure Cloud.

96
00:06:24.558 --> 00:06:25.870
Third, collect.

97
00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:29.558
The kit's query starts from Learn's
sample for all active events.

98
00:06:29.558 --> 00:06:33.256
Fourth, alert between collections
with Learn's built-in policy,

99
00:06:33.256 --> 00:06:36.670
assigned at the management
group with a remediation task.

100
00:06:36.670 --> 00:06:38.171
The kit writes it as a plan,

101
00:06:38.171 --> 00:06:42.110
and applies it only with the
hash the change record approved.

102
00:06:45.860 --> 00:06:49.849
Here's what the kit really prints,
from the workbook's first chapter,

103
00:06:49.849 --> 00:06:52.097
on fictional fixtures with no tenant.

104
00:06:52.097 --> 00:06:55.060
One collection gives five
notices from six rows:

105
00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:59.134
an incident that appeared in two
subscriptions became one notice.

106
00:06:59.134 --> 00:07:01.560
Every record is valid against the schema.

107
00:07:01.560 --> 00:07:04.198
The records show the two that need action.

108
00:07:04.198 --> 00:07:09.592
The retirement of basic public IP
addresses is due September 30,

109
00:07:09.592 --> 00:07:11.322
2027, by the kit's rule.

110
00:07:11.322 --> 00:07:16.722
The security advisory needs action with no
date, for the security officer to review.

111
00:07:16.722 --> 00:07:21.092
The incident, the advisory and the
maintenance window need no action,

112
00:07:21.092 --> 00:07:23.161
so they'll go to the weekly digest.

113
00:07:23.161 --> 00:07:28.147
Then the alert plan: two steps, the
policy assignment at the management group

114
00:07:28.147 --> 00:07:32.660
and its remediation, the plan's hash,
and a dry run that changes nothing.

115
00:07:32.660 --> 00:07:36.560
And in fixture mode, the script
refuses to apply at all.

116
00:07:40.310 --> 00:07:41.723
What does gate one support?

117
00:07:41.723 --> 00:07:43.647
Three things in NIST's catalog.

118
00:07:43.647 --> 00:07:48.273
Monitoring an external provider's
control compliance on an ongoing basis.

119
00:07:48.273 --> 00:07:52.697
Receiving security alerts and
advisories from outside organizations.

120
00:07:52.697 --> 00:07:57.348
And continuous monitoring, through the
alert path that runs between collections.

121
00:07:57.348 --> 00:07:59.775
The key security indicators are FedRAMP's,

122
00:07:59.775 --> 00:08:03.659
and which record supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

123
00:08:03.659 --> 00:08:07.156
The security officer keeps the
raw Azure file, the notices,

124
00:08:07.156 --> 00:08:10.288
and the manifest with every
call and its permission,

125
00:08:10.288 --> 00:08:14.148
plus the alert plan and the change
record that approved its hash.

126
00:08:14.148 --> 00:08:19.201
As a side note, Azure Government has its
own CLI cloud and Resource Manager host,

127
00:08:19.201 --> 00:08:23.710
and Learn hasn't stated the table's
availability there in so many words.

128
00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:25.854
Until the reader identity exists,

129
00:08:25.854 --> 00:08:29.835
a person with Reader runs Learn's
own query and keeps the output.

130
00:08:29.835 --> 00:08:32.555
And rollback: the collection only reads,

131
00:08:32.555 --> 00:08:35.880
and the alert policy
has a planned rollback.

132
00:08:39.630 --> 00:08:42.592
Gate two is Microsoft 365.

133
00:08:42.592 --> 00:08:46.780
The message center is where Microsoft
announces changes, retirements and

134
00:08:46.780 --> 00:08:50.831
actions for a tenant, and service
health lists incidents and advisories.

135
00:08:50.831 --> 00:08:54.980
Microsoft Graph's service
communications API reads both.

136
00:08:54.980 --> 00:08:59.480
Two steps: sign in to the tenant's
own cloud with two read permissions,

137
00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.855
and collect three lists,
following every next link.

138
00:09:02.855 --> 00:09:07.562
Microsoft Learn says both permissions
are granted only by an administrator,

139
00:09:07.562 --> 00:09:10.430
and a delegated sign-in
also needs an admin role.

140
00:09:10.430 --> 00:09:13.595
Data privacy messages are
visible only to Global

141
00:09:13.595 --> 00:09:16.992
Administrators and Message
Center Privacy Readers.

142
00:09:16.992 --> 00:09:18.055
Now the cloud.

143
00:09:18.055 --> 00:09:21.423
A GCC tenant keeps the
worldwide Graph endpoint,

144
00:09:21.423 --> 00:09:24.455
which Learn's national
cloud page confirms.

145
00:09:24.455 --> 00:09:28.093
GCC High and the Defense
Department have their own hosts,

146
00:09:28.093 --> 00:09:31.730
and the workbook's capture shows
the kit's map of all three.

147
00:09:31.730 --> 00:09:34.946
The kit sends each call only
to the cloud's own host,

148
00:09:34.946 --> 00:09:38.920
and refuses a session in
another environment or tenant.

149
00:09:42.670 --> 00:09:48.608
On the fixtures, Microsoft 365 gives six
notices: a retirement with a provider date,

150
00:09:48.608 --> 00:09:52.188
a change the kit marks
action required by its rule,

151
00:09:52.188 --> 00:09:54.633
three advisories and an incident.

152
00:09:54.633 --> 00:09:57.382
The next capture shows the
difference that matters.

153
00:09:57.382 --> 00:10:02.426
The retirement of basic authentication
for SMTP client submission is action

154
00:10:02.426 --> 00:10:07.082
required because Microsoft said so,
in its action-required-by date field.

155
00:10:07.082 --> 00:10:10.446
The SharePoint Framework change
is action required because

156
00:10:10.446 --> 00:10:13.282
of a stated kit rule about
a message center category.

157
00:10:13.282 --> 00:10:14.683
The record says which,

158
00:10:14.683 --> 00:10:18.883
so nobody argues with Microsoft
about a date Microsoft never set.

159
00:10:18.883 --> 00:10:22.272
This gate supports the same
two controls as gate one,

160
00:10:22.272 --> 00:10:27.395
plus configuration change control: a
change Microsoft announces is reviewed,

161
00:10:27.395 --> 00:10:29.996
with an impact analysis, before it lands.

162
00:10:29.996 --> 00:10:34.320
Until the reader exists, Learn's
own cmdlet lists the same posts.

163
00:10:34.320 --> 00:10:38.410
And there's nothing to roll
back: the collection only reads.

164
00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:44.936
Gate three is Oracle Cloud Infrastructure,

165
00:10:44.936 --> 00:10:49.022
and it matters here because the
HR platform runs on Oracle Cloud.

166
00:10:49.022 --> 00:10:52.146
Reading that tenancy's
announcements means platform

167
00:10:52.146 --> 00:10:55.547
maintenance reaches the HR
system's owners before it lands.

168
00:10:55.547 --> 00:11:00.184
Oracle publishes maintenance, changes,
action-required notices and production

169
00:11:00.184 --> 00:11:04.198
events as announcements, and updates
to one announcement form a chain.

170
00:11:04.198 --> 00:11:07.097
The kit keeps only the
latest in each chain.

171
00:11:07.097 --> 00:11:10.220
Oracle's documentation
gives the policy statement:

172
00:11:10.220 --> 00:11:13.923
allow the reader's group to read
announcements in the tenancy.

173
00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:17.971
Read, not inspect, because
inspect lists summaries only,

174
00:11:17.971 --> 00:11:20.347
without the affected resources.

175
00:11:20.347 --> 00:11:24.035
Then cross-check with Oracle's
own list command, and collect.

176
00:11:24.035 --> 00:11:28.041
For government, Oracle's US
Government Cloud page lists the

177
00:11:28.041 --> 00:11:32.373
Announcements endpoints in two
regions, in the realm called OC2.

178
00:11:32.373 --> 00:11:35.119
The third realm's
endpoint isn't specified,

179
00:11:35.119 --> 00:11:39.790
because Oracle's page wasn't readable,
so the kit refuses that realm.

180
00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:45.578
Here's Oracle on the fixtures.

181
00:11:45.578 --> 00:11:48.427
Day one gives four notices, one per chain.

182
00:11:48.427 --> 00:11:51.616
The maintenance names a
database system and its window.

183
00:11:51.616 --> 00:11:54.649
The change is action
required, by December 15,

184
00:11:54.649 --> 00:11:59.978
because Oracle's own action-required fields
say so, and it names a load balancer.

185
00:11:59.978 --> 00:12:03.565
A week later, the provider has
rescheduled the maintenance.

186
00:12:03.565 --> 00:12:08.024
Because the kit keeps only the latest
in each chain, the new announcement

187
00:12:08.024 --> 00:12:12.928
replaces the original, with the new window,
rather than becoming a second notice.

188
00:12:12.928 --> 00:12:16.140
That's exactly what the guide's
try-it says to look for.

189
00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:19.678
Beyond monitoring the provider
and receiving advisories,

190
00:12:19.678 --> 00:12:24.372
this gate supports contingency planning:
maintenance that names the agency's

191
00:12:24.372 --> 00:12:27.116
resources prompts a
contingency plan review,

192
00:12:27.116 --> 00:12:29.427
and the task carries a flag that says so.

193
00:12:29.427 --> 00:12:33.780
Until the reader exists, Oracle's
announcement email is the interim path,

194
00:12:33.780 --> 00:12:36.760
and the settings file
names who receives it.

195
00:12:40.510 --> 00:12:44.764
Gate four is AWS Health, which
publishes issues, scheduled changes

196
00:12:44.764 --> 00:12:49.172
and account notifications per
account, with the affected resources.

197
00:12:49.172 --> 00:12:51.235
It's the one provider with a cost.

198
00:12:51.235 --> 00:12:55.629
AWS's documentation says the account
needs a qualifying support plan,

199
00:12:55.629 --> 00:12:59.872
and without one the API answers
with a subscription-required error.

200
00:12:59.872 --> 00:13:03.261
AWS's own pages name the
qualifying plans differently,

201
00:13:03.261 --> 00:13:06.198
so check the account
against the current pages.

202
00:13:06.198 --> 00:13:10.765
The reader holds a customer managed
policy with three describe actions,

203
00:13:10.765 --> 00:13:14.646
because AWS publishes no read-only
managed policy for Health,

204
00:13:14.646 --> 00:13:17.386
and its full-access
policy includes writes.

205
00:13:17.386 --> 00:13:19.745
Then cross-check from the Health endpoint,

206
00:13:19.745 --> 00:13:24.398
which is US East for commercial accounts
and the GovCloud West region for GovCloud.

207
00:13:24.398 --> 00:13:25.373
And collect.

208
00:13:25.373 --> 00:13:26.998
Until the reader exists,

209
00:13:26.998 --> 00:13:31.548
an EventBridge rule on AWS Health
events pushes scheduled changes.

210
00:13:31.548 --> 00:13:37.380
Without a support plan, the AWS Health
Dashboard and its email are the only path.

211
00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:45.189
On the fixtures, AWS gives
five notices: two retirements,

212
00:13:45.189 --> 00:13:50.218
a maintenance window, a security
notification and a closed incident.

213
00:13:50.218 --> 00:13:51.380
Three need action.

214
00:13:51.380 --> 00:13:52.618
Look at the basis.

215
00:13:52.618 --> 00:13:57.344
The EC2 instance retirement and
the IAM security notification are

216
00:13:57.344 --> 00:14:01.817
action required because AWS said
so, in its actionability field.

217
00:14:01.817 --> 00:14:05.645
The Lambda lifecycle event is
action required by the kit's rule:

218
00:14:05.645 --> 00:14:10.281
a scheduled change whose event code names
a retirement, due at its start time.

219
00:14:10.281 --> 00:14:14.868
Again, the record tells you which is
AWS's word and which is the kit's.

220
00:14:14.868 --> 00:14:19.583
And the kit holds AWS's documented
limit: the details call takes one

221
00:14:19.583 --> 00:14:24.556
to ten event identifiers, so eleven
is refused before anything is sent.

222
00:14:24.556 --> 00:14:27.284
Beyond monitoring and
receiving advisories,

223
00:14:27.284 --> 00:14:31.880
this gate supports implementing directives
in the established time frames:

224
00:14:31.880 --> 00:14:35.620
an action-required event becomes
a task with its due date.

225
00:14:39.370 --> 00:14:43.932
To close Part 1, here's what the four
collection gates leave in the evidence file.

226
00:14:43.932 --> 00:14:46.539
Each one keeps the
provider's raw response,

227
00:14:46.539 --> 00:14:51.082
the normalized notices and a manifest
with every call and its permission.

228
00:14:51.082 --> 00:14:53.894
Azure adds the alert plan and its results.

229
00:14:53.894 --> 00:14:58.577
Together they support monitoring external
providers, receiving their advisories,

230
00:14:58.577 --> 00:15:02.925
continuous monitoring, configuration
change control for announced changes,

231
00:15:02.925 --> 00:15:05.333
and contingency planning for maintenance.

232
00:15:05.333 --> 00:15:09.817
Part 2 picks up from there: Google
Cloud's incidents and its maintenance gap,

233
00:15:09.817 --> 00:15:14.302
then routing every notice that needs
action to an owner as a task, the weekly

234
00:15:14.302 --> 00:15:17.774
digest, and the evidence chain
